# Source: HoareLogic Exercises.pdf ("ICT3101 Exercises, Deductive Verification"), problem 4 b), with its solution in "HoareLogic Exercises Solutions - Problem 4.pdf".
id: prove-sequence-b
title: Three assignments with logical variables (b)
topic: Sequential composition
level: 2
source: HoareLogic Exercises, problem 4 b
tags: assignment, sequence, logical variables, signs

task:
  Calculate the weakest precondition of the program for the postcondition
  $Q$, statement by statement from the last one. Then use the consequence
  rule to decide whether the triple is valid.

  The variables $x'$ and $y'$ are logical variables: they record the
  initial values of $x$ and $y$, and the program does not change them.
  The program does not assign $n$ and $m$ either.

pre: x = x' && y = y'
post: x = -m && y = x' + n + y'

program:
  {P}
  {R3}
  x := x + y;            // i1
  {R2}
  y := x + n;            // i2
  {R1}
  x := x - y + n - m     // i3
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of x := x - y + n - m
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i3
text:
  The precondition of an assignment is obtained by substituting the
  assigned variable ($x$) with the assignment expression ($x - y + n - m$)
  in the postcondition $Q$. $x'$ is a different variable and does not
  change.
calc:
  wp(x := x - y + n - m, Q)
  = (x = -m && y = x' + n + y')[x <- x - y + n - m]
  = x - y + n - m = -m && y = x' + n + y'     // the x of Q is replaced by x - y + n - m
set: R1 = x - y + n - m = -m && y = x' + n + y'

step: R2, the weakest precondition of y := x + n
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($y$) with the assignment expression
  ($x + n$) in the postcondition of this statement, which is $R1$. $y$
  occurs twice in $R1$, so both occurrences are replaced. The parentheses
  matter: the minus in front of $y$ applies to the whole of $x + n$.
calc:
  wp(y := x + n, R1)
  = (x - y + n - m = -m && y = x' + n + y')[y <- x + n]
  = x - (x + n) + n - m = -m && x + n = x' + n + y'   // both y of R1 are replaced by x + n
  = x - x - n + n - m = -m && x + n = x' + n + y'     // the minus in front of (x + n) changes the sign of x and of n
  = -m = -m && x + n = x' + n + y'                    // x - x and -n + n cancel
  = true && x + n = x' + n + y'                       // -m = -m has the form A = A, so it is true
  = x + n = x' + n + y'                               // true && A is A
  = x = x' + y'                                       // subtract n from both sides
set: R2 = x = x' + y'

step: R3, the weakest precondition of x := x + y
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($x$) with the assignment expression
  ($x + y$) in the postcondition of this statement, which is $R2$.
calc:
  wp(x := x + y, R2)
  = (x = x' + y')[x <- x + y]
  = x + y = x' + y'      // the x of R2 is replaced by x + y
set: R3 = x + y = x' + y'

step: P implies R3
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: P, R3
text:
  $R3$ is the weakest precondition of the whole program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R3$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R3»
vc: x = x' && y = y' ==> x + y = x' + y'
why:
  Assume $P$. Replace $x$ by $x'$ and $y$ by $y'$ in $R3$. It becomes
  $x' + y' = x' + y'$, which is true.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The most frequent mistake in this exercise is the sign error
  $x - (x + n) = x - x + n$. The correct expansion is $x - x - n$, and
  this is what makes the terms $n$ cancel.
